    "diagnosticNotes": [
      "Antibiotic prophylaxis refers to the administration of antibiotics in patients to reduce the risk of perioperative sepsis. The main cause of morbidity and mortality in surgery is infectious complications. Antibiotic prophylaxis is indicated in cases where sepsis is expected and could have disastrous local or generalised effects. For prophylaxis to be effective, antibiotics must be given before contamination takes place or at the earliest possible time before infection is established. For surgery, therefore, it must be given IV about the time of induction of anaesthesia, so that tissues are saturated with the antibiotic before contamination occurs."
    ]
